 she did nothing differently from  these other celebrities  other than just be herself to be  publicly assigned that role  to dive a little bit deeper into these  issues i brought on a body image coach  and content creator danny of i'm a pound  cake  thanks for joining me danny hi thank you  for having me  so why do you think the public's  reaction to lizzo's  detox was so different from the public's  reaction to other larger bodied white  celebrities like  rubble wilson and adele well i think a  lot of it has to do with that lizzo is  black right  and a lot of the media representation  that we've seen of that black women  um has been controlled uh by the media  and lizzo living her her life and in a  way that  um people are not used to was triggering  because she is definitely a fat black  woman and  we even though there's a lot of fat  phobia and we  we experience that in our society based  on all different  sizes when that is coupled with  blackness there's a layer  uh that gets confusing for people and  then we see  uh this racial undertone comes out and  in the way that we treat larger bodies  okay and  and and what do you think about lizzo  being painted as like the poster child  of the body positive movement i think  it's unfair i think there's a lot of  things that happen right i think that  lizzo also participated um  in kind of being the poster child um but  also  uh this we always do this to black women  also right like we make black women our  martyrs we make black women the  prototype of the  prototype of the thing but we don't give  them the agency that they're asking for  we don't give them  the chance to make public mistakes or  live their lives in ways that we allow  white women of any size to live their  life right and  we never see any backlash  to white women in the same way that we  see it to black  uh black women and we think uh black  women who are larger who are special  like fat black women we expect for them  to be our mammy that trope  was created by white uh westerners  um who created this idea that uh fat  black women are our mothers they're  supposed to nurture  uh this entire uh revolution  any revolution right and be our  caretakers  um which is unfair it's rooted in racism  um it's a problem and it's something  that i think people in the body  positivity movement  should call out and that's a little bit  of my issue with this movement right  we we only talk about bodies from a lens  of size and eating disorders  and we never get down to race and then  the ways that  fat black bodies have been positioned in  society  especially when they have been femme a  lot of the racist images  of fat black women are not talked about  in the body positivity  movement and so it's hard for people to  understand  um what they're doing to lizzo i think  people  know what they're doing but i don't  think people understand how to explain  what is happening  okay so why and how is race an important  part of the body positive movement  i think it's really important that  people who are part of this movement  um see fat black people as people who  are treated differently not just because  of size  but also because of race um because  we do not get to think of our bodies as  just the way up about the way that  we don't get to think of our bodies as a  function right and what they can do for  us  it's also a different experience when i  walk in the store  in this body and how people may follow  me because i live in this body  and that's also a body positivity issue  the death of brianna taylor is a body  positive issue the the death of george  floyd is a body positive  issue um because we don't get to  separate our size  um from the way that we exist in this  world due to our race  wow that's so beautiful and just  so enlightening for i'm sure everyone  who's watching so  thank you so much for sharing that i  really appreciate your time  um and i hope you have a fantastic new  year  thank you have a good day so you can  check out where to find danny in the  description below  now a few final thoughts on lizzo's role  in the body positive space  first of all a lot of you may be  wondering if lizzo's  detox tick tock means that she should  lose her crown as  queen of the bopo movement well  much like what i discussed in my video  on cassie from blog lattes here  lizzo actually never signed up for the  gig  she just sought out to share her musical  talent with the world  and give some amazing live and recorded  performances  and it just so happens that she's doing  it while living  in a fat black body that should not be  her defining feature  and the fact that it is even by her  biggest fans  is evidence of a lot of underlying  racism and fat phobia  in our society so posting those tick  tocks does not make lizzo  antibody positive and we shouldn't be  mad at lizzo  her desire to lose weight or get healthy  whatever her motivation is  is no less valid than a white woman's  desire or goal  also tearing women down for being at  different points in their body positive  journey  is not helping the problem it doesn't  change the societal pressures  of our aesthetic driven culture it just  shames women  for being too powerless to overcome them  so robbing lizzo of the same grace and  liberties that we afford  other larger bodied women is a problem  and it diverts the attention away from  what we should be really working towards  as a society  and that is dismantling diet culture  debunking nutrition misinformation  and examining our own internal biases  rather than  other people's individual choices and on  that note  that is all that i have for today thank  you again to bill barr for sponsoring  this video  and for danny for her amazing insight if  you like this video  don't forget to give it the thumbs up  leave me a comment below with some of  your thoughts on the lisso detox  fiasco subscribe to the channel and i'll  see you next time on abby's kitchen  bye  [Music]  you
 

